WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.155
Risk management connects security
conditions with organizational objectives and

2
00:00:04.235 --> 00:00:05.625
decisions about what to do next.

3
00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:08.433
Inputs include asset and service
inventories, threat models,

4
00:00:08.513 --> 00:00:12.425
vulnerability findings, incident history,
control assessments, and business context.

5
00:00:12.505 --> 00:00:14.925
AI can reconcile these records, draft risk
statements,

6
00:00:15.005 --> 00:00:16.394
and compare treatment options.

7
00:00:16.474 --> 00:00:19.769
The useful output is a supported decision
record with an accountable owner,

8
00:00:19.849 --> 00:00:22.165
rather than a severity score without an
explanation.

9
00:00:22.245 --> 00:00:23.532
Describe a risk as a scenario:

10
00:00:23.612 --> 00:00:26.392
a threat could exploit a weakness under
particular conditions,

11
00:00:26.472 --> 00:00:29.046
causing a consequence for a service or
business objective.

12
00:00:29.126 --> 00:00:31.030
Separate observed facts from assumptions.

13
00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:34.045
For example, a payroll service may depend
on one identity provider.

14
00:00:34.125 --> 00:00:36.801
An extended identity outage could delay
payroll processing,

15
00:00:36.881 --> 00:00:39.558
but the impact depends on deadlines,
recovery arrangements,

16
00:00:39.638 --> 00:00:40.873
and available workarounds.

17
00:00:40.953 --> 00:00:44.248
AI can organize those dependencies and
draft questions for service owners;

18
00:00:44.328 --> 00:00:47.159
it cannot infer the answers from a generic
industry description.

19
00:00:47.239 --> 00:00:50.431
Assess likelihood and impact using the
organization's agreed criteria.

20
00:00:50.511 --> 00:00:53.342
Ask the model to identify the evidence
supporting each estimate,

21
00:00:53.422 --> 00:00:56.613
the uncertainties, and the conditions that
would change the assessment.

22
00:00:56.693 --> 00:00:58.804
Avoid false precision when evidence is
limited.

23
00:00:58.884 --> 00:01:02.178
A technical severity rating is one input:
exposure, business criticality,

24
00:01:02.258 --> 00:01:05.296
existing controls, and recovery capability
can change the priority.

25
00:01:05.376 --> 00:01:08.670
Review the assessment with people who
understand the affected operations.

26
00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.839
Compare treatment options such as reducing
the risk through controls,

27
00:01:11.919 --> 00:01:15.573
avoiding the activity, sharing some
consequences through contracts or insurance,

28
00:01:15.653 --> 00:01:18.227
or accepting the residual risk within
approved authority.

29
00:01:18.307 --> 00:01:21.241
AI can draft options with expected
benefits, costs, dependencies,

30
00:01:21.321 --> 00:01:23.175
and evidence needed to verify completion.

31
00:01:23.255 --> 00:01:24.284
In the payroll example,

32
00:01:24.364 --> 00:01:28.122
compare recovery improvements and tested
contingency procedures against the likely

33
00:01:28.202 --> 00:01:30.055
interruption and implementation effort.

34
00:01:30.135 --> 00:01:33.378
A drafted recommendation does not
authorize spending or accept risk on the

35
00:01:33.458 --> 00:01:34.539
organization's behalf.

36
00:01:34.619 --> 00:01:37.913
Maintain a risk register that records the
scenario, evidence, assessment,

37
00:01:37.993 --> 00:01:40.207
treatment, owner, target date, and
residual risk.

38
00:01:40.287 --> 00:01:43.427
Add review dates and triggers such as a
control failure, service change,

39
00:01:43.507 --> 00:01:44.228
or new incident.

40
00:01:44.308 --> 00:01:46.315
AI can flag stale records, summarize
changes,

41
00:01:46.395 --> 00:01:48.197
and prepare updates for decision makers.

42
00:01:48.277 --> 00:01:50.696
Accountable people approve treatment and
acceptance,

43
00:01:50.776 --> 00:01:54.483
while follow-up checks establish whether
the planned controls actually reduced the

44
00:01:54.563 --> 00:01:54.820
risk.
